  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sun Jun 21 2026\n\nMid/upper high center firmly in place now over the Sonoran and \nChihuahua Deserts of Mexico with ridges extending east and west \nfrom the center. Some very high level moisture continues to drift \neastward over the top of the ridge across much of New Mexico. \nDriest air with respect to PW is over northern New Mexico, but \nonly slightly higher over our CWA of southern New Mexico/West \nTexas. PWs range from .30 inches north to .70 inches far \nsoutheast, while 500mb temps are very warm at around -1C to -2C \ndegrees. Little change in any of these features through Tuesday, \nmeaning little in the way of clouds, let alone any chance of rain.\nHeat will continue to build with lowland highs today just below \nAdvisory criteria (105 deg) and Monday just at criteria for much \nof the lowlands. \n\nTuesday through Friday...subtle changes begin in this period. The\nupper high loses its elliptical shape and becomes more circular, \nthus allowing some sub-tropical moisture to advect in. At mid \nlevels, 500mb temps cool down to around -5C to -7C degrees, \nenabling slight instability of the airmass to redevelop. The \nwesterly flow aloft across the top of the high may also allow weak\ndisturbances to track eastward across the CWA. All this to say a \nslight chance of afternoon thunderstorms return to the mountains \nWednesday through Friday, and to the lowlands toward evening \nThursday and Friday. Despite the increase of cloud buildups and \nchance of rain, heat will continue to build. Tuesday and Wednesday\nlook to be the peak heating, with lowland high temps ranging from\n103-108 deg. Will likely extend Heat Advisory into Tue/Wed with \nissuance this afternoon. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...upper high sinks a bit further south into \nMexico, allowing the Polar jet to drop over the southern Rockies \nwith much drier air, ending any chance of rain. This will also \nlikely bring some breezy afternoon winds each day, elevating fire \nconcerns.",
